/**
@page api_reliability Reliability Metrics and Node Failure/Repair
@brief Node breakdown/repair modeling and reliability metrics computation in random environments

This module provides support for modeling node failures and repairs in queueing networks
and computing standard reliability metrics such as MTTF, MTTR, MTBF, and availability.

@section reliability_overview Overview

The `Env` (Environment) class in LINE supports modeling random environments where nodes
can fail (break down) and be repaired. This allows analysis of systems with unreliable
resources, such as:
- Manufacturing systems with machine failures
- Computing systems with server crashes
- Communication networks with link failures
- Service systems with operator unavailability

The reliability modeling framework provides:
- **Node breakdown**: Transition from operational (UP) to failed (DOWN) state
- **Node repair**: Transition from failed (DOWN) back to operational (UP) state
- **Custom reset policies**: Control queue behavior during state transitions
- **Reliability metrics**: MTTF, MTTR, MTBF, and steady-state availability

@section reliability_concepts Key Concepts

@subsection reliability_stages Environment Stages

An environment consists of stages representing different operational states:
- **UP stage**: Node is operational with normal service rate
- **DOWN_nodename stages**: Node is failed with degraded/zero service rate

Transitions between stages model random breakdowns and repairs.

@subsection reliability_metrics Standard Metrics

<table>
<tr><th>Metric</th><th>Name</th><th>Definition</th></tr>
<tr><td>MTTF</td><td>Mean Time To Failure</td><td>Expected time until breakdown from UP state</td></tr>
<tr><td>MTTR</td><td>Mean Time To Repair</td><td>Expected time to restore from DOWN state</td></tr>
<tr><td>MTBF</td><td>Mean Time Between Failures</td><td>MTTF + MTTR</td></tr>
<tr><td>Availability</td><td>Steady-State Availability</td><td>Fraction of time system is operational (P(UP))</td></tr>
</table>

For systems with multiple failure modes (competing risks):
- MTTF uses combined failure rate: λ_total = Σ λ_i
- MTTR is weighted average based on steady-state probabilities of DOWN states

@section reliability_usage Usage

@subsection reliability_matlab MATLAB

<b>Basic Node Failure/Repair:</b>
@code
% Create base network
model = Network('ReliabilityExample');
source = Source(model, 'Source');
queue = Queue(model, 'Server', SchedStrategy.FCFS);
sink = Sink(model, 'Sink');

oclass = OpenClass(model, 'Jobs', 0);
source.setArrival(oclass, Exp(0.5));
queue.setService(oclass, Exp(2.0));  % Normal service rate

model.link(Network.serialRouting(source, queue, sink));

% Create environment with breakdown and repair
env = Env('ServerEnvironment');

% Add failure and repair for 'Server' node
% Breakdown rate: λ = 0.1 (MTTF = 10)
% Repair rate: μ = 1.0 (MTTR = 1)
% Service during failure: rate = 0.5 (degraded, or use Disabled())
env.addNodeFailureRepair(model, 'Server', Exp(0.1), Exp(1.0), Exp(0.5));

% Solve the environment
solver = SolverCTMC(env);
solver.run();

% Get reliability metrics
RT = env.getReliabilityTable();
disp(RT);
@endcode

<b>Separate Breakdown and Repair:</b>
@code
% Create environment
env = Env('CustomEnvironment');

% Add breakdown only
env.addNodeBreakdown(model, 'Server', Exp(0.1), Disabled());

% Add repair separately
env.addNodeRepair('Server', Exp(1.0));
@endcode

<b>Multiple Failure Modes:</b>
@code
% Add different types of failures for same node
env.addNodeBreakdown(model, 'Server', Exp(0.1), Exp(0.5));  % Soft failure
env.addNodeBreakdown(model, 'Server', Exp(0.05), Disabled());  % Hard failure
env.addNodeRepair('Server', Exp(1.0));  % Common repair
@endcode

@subsection reliability_java Java/Kotlin

<b>Basic Node Failure/Repair:</b>
@code
// Create base network
Network model = new Network("ReliabilityExample");
Source source = new Source(model, "Source");
Queue queue = new Queue(model, "Server", SchedStrategy.FCFS);
Sink sink = new Sink(model, "Sink");

OpenClass oclass = new OpenClass(model, "Jobs", 0);
source.setArrival(oclass, new Exp(0.5));
queue.setService(oclass, new Exp(2.0));  // Normal service rate

model.link(Network.serialRouting(source, queue, sink));

// Create environment with breakdown and repair
Env env = new Env("ServerEnvironment");

// Add failure and repair for 'Server' node
// Breakdown rate: λ = 0.1 (MTTF = 10)
// Repair rate: μ = 1.0 (MTTR = 1)
// Service during failure: rate = 0.5 (degraded)
env.addNodeFailureRepair(model, "Server", new Exp(0.1), new Exp(1.0), new Exp(0.5));

// Solve the environment
SolverCTMC solver = new SolverCTMC(env);
solver.run();

// Get reliability metrics
Map<String, Double> metrics = env.getReliabilityTable();
System.out.println("MTTF: " + metrics.get("MTTF"));
System.out.println("MTTR: " + metrics.get("MTTR"));
System.out.println("MTBF: " + metrics.get("MTBF"));
System.out.println("Availability: " + metrics.get("Availability"));
@endcode

<b>Separate Breakdown and Repair:</b>
@code
// Create environment
Env env = new Env("CustomEnvironment");

// Add breakdown only
env.addNodeBreakdown(model, "Server", new Exp(0.1), new Disabled());

// Add repair separately
env.addNodeRepair("Server", new Exp(1.0));
@endcode

@subsection reliability_python Python

<b>Basic Node Failure/Repair:</b>
@code
from line_solver import *

# Create base network
model = Network('ReliabilityExample')
source = Source(model, 'Source')
queue = Queue(model, 'Server', SchedStrategy.FCFS)
sink = Sink(model, 'Sink')

oclass = OpenClass(model, 'Jobs', 0)
source.setArrival(oclass, Exp(0.5))
queue.setService(oclass, Exp(2.0))  # Normal service rate

model.link(Network.serialRouting(source, queue, sink))

# Create environment with breakdown and repair
env = Env('ServerEnvironment')

# Add failure and repair for 'Server' node
# Breakdown rate: λ = 0.1 (MTTF = 10)
# Repair rate: μ = 1.0 (MTTR = 1)
# Service during failure: rate = 0.5 (degraded)
env.addNodeFailureRepair(model, 'Server', Exp(0.1), Exp(1.0), Exp(0.5))

# Solve the environment
solver = SolverCTMC(env)
solver.run()

# Get reliability metrics
RT = env.getReliabilityTable()
print(RT)
@endcode

@section reliability_api API Reference

@subsection reliability_env_methods Env Class Methods

<b>addNodeFailureRepair(baseModel, nodeName, breakdownDist, repairDist, downServiceDist)</b>

Convenience method to add both breakdown and repair for a node.

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
env.addNodeFailureRepair(model, nodeName, breakdownDist, repairDist, downServiceDist)
@endcode

<b>Parameters:</b>
<table>
<tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>baseModel</td><td>Network</td><td>Base network model containing the node</td></tr>
<tr><td>nodeName</td><td>String</td><td>Name of the node that can fail and be repaired</td></tr>
<tr><td>breakdownDist</td><td>Markovian</td><td>Distribution for time until breakdown (UP→DOWN)</td></tr>
<tr><td>repairDist</td><td>Markovian</td><td>Distribution for repair time (DOWN→UP)</td></tr>
<tr><td>downServiceDist</td><td>Markovian</td><td>Service distribution when node is DOWN (use Disabled() for no service)</td></tr>
</table>

<b>addNodeBreakdown(baseModel, nodeName, breakdownDist, downServiceDist)</b>

Adds a breakdown transition for a node.

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
env.addNodeBreakdown(model, nodeName, breakdownDist, downServiceDist)
@endcode

<b>Parameters:</b>
<table>
<tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>baseModel</td><td>Network</td><td>Base network model</td></tr>
<tr><td>nodeName</td><td>String</td><td>Name of the node that can break down</td></tr>
<tr><td>breakdownDist</td><td>Markovian</td><td>Distribution for time until breakdown</td></tr>
<tr><td>downServiceDist</td><td>Markovian</td><td>Service distribution when DOWN</td></tr>
</table>

<b>addNodeRepair(nodeName, repairDist)</b>

Adds a repair transition for a previously added breakdown.

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
env.addNodeRepair(nodeName, repairDist)
@endcode

<b>Parameters:</b>
<table>
<tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>nodeName</td><td>String</td><td>Name of the node to repair</td></tr>
<tr><td>repairDist</td><td>Markovian</td><td>Distribution for repair time</td></tr>
</table>

<b>getReliabilityTable()</b>

Computes and returns reliability metrics.

<b>Aliases:</b> \c relT, \c getRelT, \c relTable, \c getRelTable

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
RT = env.getReliabilityTable()  % MATLAB - returns table
RT = env.relT()                 % MATLAB - short alias
Map<String, Double> metrics = env.getReliabilityTable();  // Java
Map<String, Double> metrics = env.relT();                 // Java - short alias
RT = env.getReliabilityTable()  # Python - returns table/dict
@endcode

<b>Returns:</b>
<table>
<tr><th>Key</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>"MTTF"</td><td>Mean Time To Failure</td></tr>
<tr><td>"MTTR"</td><td>Mean Time To Repair</td></tr>
<tr><td>"MTBF"</td><td>Mean Time Between Failures (MTTF + MTTR)</td></tr>
<tr><td>"Availability"</td><td>Steady-state availability (fraction of time in UP state)</td></tr>
</table>

@section reliability_advanced Advanced Features

@subsection reliability_reset Custom Reset Policies

By default, queue lengths are preserved during state transitions. Custom reset policies
can be specified:

@code
% MATLAB: Reset queue to empty on breakdown
resetToEmpty = @(input) input * 0;
env.addNodeBreakdown(model, 'Server', Exp(0.1), Disabled(), resetToEmpty);

// Java: Reset queue to empty
env.addNodeBreakdown(model, "Server", new Exp(0.1), new Disabled(),
    input -> input.mult(0));
@endcode

@subsection reliability_competing_risks Multiple Failure Modes

When a node has multiple failure modes, the total failure rate follows competing risks:

@code
% Example: Hardware (λ=0.1) and software (λ=0.05) failures
env.addNodeBreakdown(model, 'Server', Exp(0.1), Disabled());  % Hardware
env.addNodeBreakdown(model, 'Server', Exp(0.05), Exp(0.1));  % Software (degraded)
env.addNodeRepair('Server', Exp(1.0));  % Common repair

% Total failure rate: λ_total = 0.1 + 0.05 = 0.15
% MTTF = 1/0.15 = 6.67
@endcode

The `getReliabilityTable()` method automatically handles:
- Combined failure rates
- Weighted MTTR based on DOWN state probabilities
- Correct availability calculation

@section reliability_formulas Mathematical Formulas

@subsection reliability_single_mode Single Failure Mode

For a system with one failure mode:
\f[
\text{MTTF} = \frac{1}{\lambda}
\f]
\f[
\text{MTTR} = \frac{1}{\mu}
\f]
\f[
\text{MTBF} = \text{MTTF} + \text{MTTR} = \frac{1}{\lambda} + \frac{1}{\mu}
\f]
\f[
\text{Availability} = \frac{\text{MTTF}}{\text{MTBF}} = \frac{\mu}{\lambda + \mu}
\f]

where λ is the breakdown rate and μ is the repair rate.

@subsection reliability_multiple_modes Multiple Failure Modes (Competing Risks)

For n independent failure modes with rates λ₁, λ₂, ..., λₙ:
\f[
\lambda_{\text{total}} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \lambda_i
\f]
\f[
\text{MTTF} = \frac{1}{\lambda_{\text{total}}}
\f]

MTTR is computed as weighted average:
\f[
\text{MTTR} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} p_i \cdot \frac{1}{\mu_i}
\f]

where p_i is the normalized steady-state probability of DOWN state i.

@section reliability_examples Examples

<b>Example Files:</b>
- `matlab/test/test_reliability_metrics.m` - Comprehensive validation tests
- `matlab/examples/randomEnv/renv_node_breakdown_example.m` - Basic usage example
- `jar/src/main/kotlin/jline/lang/Env.java` - Java implementation and Javadoc

@section reliability_solver_support Solver Support

Reliability analysis requires solving the underlying Markov chain:
- **Primary solver:** SolverCTMC (Continuous-Time Markov Chain)
- **Alternative:** SolverSSA (State-Space Analysis) for larger models

The environment must be solved before calling `getReliabilityTable()`.

@section reliability_references References

- Trivedi, K. S. (2016). Probability and Statistics with Reliability, Queueing, and Computer Science Applications (2nd ed.). Wiley.
- Barlow, R. E., & Proschan, F. (1996). Mathematical Theory of Reliability. SIAM.
- Ross, S. M. (2014). Introduction to Probability Models (11th ed.). Academic Press.

@see Env class documentation for complete API
@see @ref api_random_environments for random environment modeling
*/
